{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013pUAZaPTN2ZfaJpRaNFvsW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Design Constitution\n2\t#\n3\t# This constitution governs the design/architecting phase: creating VISION,\n4\t# ARCHITECTURE, SRDs, use cases, test suites, and other documentation. It is\n5\t# scaffolded into consuming projects so Claude knows how to write specs that\n6\t# match the project's documentation standards.\n7\t\n8\tarticles:\n9\t  - id: D1\n10\t    title: Specification-driven development\n11\t    rule: |\n12\t      Specifications are the source of truth. Code serves specifications, not\n13\t      the other way around. No implementation code before the SRD and use case\n14\t      exist. No implementation issue before a test suite exists for its use case.\n15\t\n16\t  - id: D2\n17\t    title: YAML-first for structured docs\n18\t    rule: |\n19\t      Use YAML for structured documents (VISION, ARCHITECTURE, SRDs, use cases,\n20\t      test suites). Use markdown for prose-heavy guidelines and specifications\n21\t      summaries. YAML is machine-readable by design.\n22\t\n23\t  - id: D3\n24\t    title: Test suite linkage is mandatory\n25\t    rule: |\n26\t      Every use case must have a corresponding test suite. The test suite\n27\t      validates the use case's success criteria with explicit inputs and\n28\t      expected outputs.\n29\t\n30\t  - id: D4\n31\t    title: Traceability\n32\t    rule: |\n33\t      Every SRD traces to VISION and ARCHITECTURE. Every use case traces to\n34\t      SRDs (via touchpoints). Every test suite traces to use cases (via the\n35\t      traces field). Code traces to SRDs via commit messages and comments.\n36\t\n37\t  - id: D5\n38\t    title: Roadmap-driven releases\n39\t    rule: |\n40\t      Use cases are assigned to releases in road-map.yaml. Releases are\n41\t      numbered rel[NN].[N] (e.g., rel01.0, rel02.1). Minor releases validate\n42\t      completed major releases without renumbering existing use cases.\n43\t\n44\tdocumentation_standards:\n45\t  style: |\n46\t    Write in the style of The Elements of Style by Strunk and White: concise,\n47\t    active voice, specific and concrete language, no unnecessary words.\n48\t\n49\t  voice: |\n50\t    Use the royal \"we\" in active voice. GOOD: \"We implement the feature...\"\n51\t    BAD: \"This document describes...\" or \"The architecture implements...\"\n52\t\n53\t  forbidden_terms:\n54\t    - critical, critically, key, deliberate, strategic\n55\t    - precisely, absolutely, fundamental, breakthrough\n56\t    - principled, honest, at the heart of, grounded\n57\t    - standards-aligned (unless absolutely necessary)\n58\t\n59\t  formatting:\n60\t    paragraphs: |\n61\t      Use paragraph form unless not possible. Vary sentence length for\n62\t      readability. Use short paragraphs only to emphasize a point. DO NOT use\n63\t      bold text or horizontal rules.\n64\t\n65\t    lists_and_tables: |\n66\t      Use tables instead of lists for short entries. Name all tables. DO NOT\n67\t      use colons except to introduce lists.\n68\t\n69\t    abbreviations: |\n70\t      Explain abbreviations at least once per document section. Define\n71\t      domain-specific abbreviations on first use.\n72\t\n73\t  figures_and_diagrams: |\n74\t    All diagrams MUST be written in Mermaid and defined inline in markdown\n75\t    using fenced code blocks. Do not create separate image files.\n76\t    Use centered table format for captioning:\n77\t    ```mermaid\n78\t    graph TD\n79\t        ... diagram source ...\n80\t    ```\n81\t\n82\tdocument_types:\n83\t  vision:\n84\t    location: docs/VISION.yaml\n85\t    format_rule: vision-format\n86\t    required_fields:\n87\t      - id\n88\t      - title\n89\t      - executive_summary\n90\t      - problem\n91\t      - what_this_does\n92\t      - why_we_build_this\n93\t      - success_criteria\n94\t      - implementation_phases\n95\t      - risks\n96\t      - not (explicit boundaries)\n97\t    purpose: |\n98\t      States what the project is, why it exists, how success is measured, and\n99\t      what it is not. Orients stakeholders, new contributors, and downstream\n100\t      docs (ARCHITECTURE, SRDs).\n101\t\n102\t  architecture:\n103\t    location: docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml\n104\t    format_rule: architecture-format\n105\t    required_fields:\n106\t      - id\n107\t      - title\n108\t      - overview (summary, lifecycle, coordination_pattern)\n109\t      - interfaces (data_structures, operations, announcements)\n110\t      - components (name, provided_by, responsibility, capabilities, references)\n111\t      - design_decisions (id, title, decision, benefits, alternatives_rejected)\n112\t      - technology_choices\n113\t      - project_structure\n114\t      - implementation_status\n115\t      - related_documents\n116\t    purpose: |\n117\t      Describes how the system is built: components, interfaces, protocols,\n118\t      data flow, design decisions. Bridge between vision (what and why) and\n119\t      SRDs (numbered requirements).\n120\t\n121\t  srd:\n122\t    location: \"docs/specs/software-requirements/srd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml\"\n123\t    format_rule: srd-format\n124\t    required_fields:\n125\t      - id\n126\t      - title\n127\t      - problem (multi-line, why it matters)\n128\t      - goals (G1, G2, ...)\n129\t      - requirements (R1.1, R1.2, ..., grouped by R1, R2, ...)\n130\t      - non_goals\n131\t      - acceptance_criteria (list of objects with id, criterion, traces)\n132\t    numbering:\n133\t      goals: \"G1, G2, G3, ...\"\n134\t      requirement_groups: \"R1, R2, R3, ...\"\n135\t      requirement_items: \"R1.1, R1.2, R2.1, ...\"\n136\t      requirement_id_format: \"R{group}.{item} — numeric dotted only (e.g., R1.1, R2.3). Letter suffixes (R2a) are not valid and are flagged by mage analyze.\"\n137\t      requirement_weight: |\n138\t        Each R-item may optionally specify a weight indicating implementation\n139\t        complexity. Weight is a positive integer with no upper bound, default 1.\n140\t        The measure agent uses weights to batch requirements into tasks that fit\n141\t        within the max_weight_per_task budget. Two YAML formats are supported:\n142\t\n143\t        Simple (weight defaults to 1):\n144\t          - R1.1: Must accept -f flag\n145\t\n146\t        Weighted:\n147\t          - R6.1:\n148\t              text: Must scan each input line for known timestamp patterns\n149\t              weight: 3\n150\t      acceptance_criteria_ids: \"AC1, AC2, AC3, ... (sequential within each SRD)\"\n151\t    acceptance_criteria_schema:\n152\t      description: |\n153\t        Each acceptance criterion is a structured object linking a checkable\n154\t        statement to the requirement items it validates. Every R-item in the\n155\t        SRD must appear in at least one AC's traces list.\n156\t      fields:\n157\t        - id: \"AC1, AC2, ... (sequential)\"\n158\t        - criterion: \"Checkable statement (the prose text)\"\n159\t        - traces: \"List of R-item IDs this AC validates (e.g., [R1.1, R1.2])\"\n160\t      example: |\n161\t        acceptance_criteria:\n162\t          - id: AC1\n163\t            criterion: Config struct includes all fields listed in R1 with YAML struct tags\n164\t            traces: [R1.1, R1.2, R1.3]\n165\t      completeness_rule: \"Every R-item must appear in at least one AC's traces list\"\n166\t    purpose: |\n167\t      Defines product requirements with numbered goals and requirements. Each\n168\t      requirement starts with \"must\" or \"must not\" or a concrete verb. No scope\n169\t      creep - non_goals define explicit boundaries.\n170\t\n171\t  use_case:\n172\t    location: \"docs/specs/use-cases/rel[NN].[N]-uc[NNN]-[short-name].yaml\"\n173\t    format_rule: use-case-format\n174\t    required_fields:\n175\t      - id\n176\t      - title\n177\t      - summary\n178\t      - actor\n179\t      - trigger\n180\t      - flow (F1, F2, ..., end-to-end steps)\n181\t      - touchpoints (T1, T2, ..., architecture elements exercised)\n182\t      - success_criteria (list of objects with id, criterion, traces)\n183\t      - out_of_scope\n184\t    numbering:\n185\t      flow_steps: \"F1, F2, F3, ...\"\n186\t      touchpoints: \"T1, T2, T3, ...\"\n187\t      success_criteria: \"S1, S2, S3, ...\"\n188\t    success_criteria_schema:\n189\t      description: |\n190\t        Each success criterion is a structured object linking a checkable\n191\t        outcome to the SRD acceptance criteria it exercises. The traces field\n192\t        references SRD AC IDs using the format srdNNN-name ACN.\n193\t      fields:\n194\t        - id: \"S1, S2, ... (sequential)\"\n195\t        - criterion: \"Checkable outcome statement\"\n196\t        - traces: \"List of SRD AC IDs (e.g., [srd001-orchestrator-core AC1])\"\n197\t      example: |\n198\t        success_criteria:\n199\t          - id: S1\n200\t            criterion: New(config) returns a non-nil *Orchestrator\n201\t            traces:\n202\t              - srd001-orchestrator-core AC2\n203\t    purpose: |\n204\t      Describes a concrete usage of the architecture. One end-to-end path\n205\t      (tracer bullet) through the system. Leads to a proof-of-concept or demo.\n206\t      Every use case must be covered by its release test suite.\n207\t\n208\t  test_suite:\n209\t    location: \"docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-[release-id].yaml\"\n210\t    format_rule: test-case-format\n211\t    required_fields:\n212\t      - id (matching filename, e.g. test-rel01.0)\n213\t      - title\n214\t      - release (release ID this suite covers)\n215\t      - traces (list of use case IDs covered by this suite)\n216\t      - test_cases (list organized by use case sub-sections, each with name, inputs, expected outputs)\n217\t    traces_format: |\n218\t      The traces field in test_cases can reference three kinds of IDs:\n219\t        - SRD R-item IDs: srdNNN-name RN.N (e.g., srd001-orchestrator-core R1.1)\n220\t        - SRD AC IDs: srdNNN-name ACN (e.g., srd001-orchestrator-core AC1)\n221\t        - UC S-item IDs: relNN.N-ucNNN-name SN (e.g., rel01.0-uc001-orchestrator-initialization S1)\n222\t    purpose: |\n223\t      Groups test cases for a full release. Each use case in the release appears\n224\t      as a sub-section within the suite. The YAML provides quick human/Claude\n225\t      consumption of test coverage; Go tests in tests/rel01.0/ use flat top-level\n226\t      functions named TestRel{NN}_UC{NNN}_DescriptiveName. See\n227\t      docs/constitutions/testing.yaml for the naming convention.\n228\t\n229\t  engineering_guideline:\n230\t    location: \"docs/engineering/eng[NN]-[short-name].yaml\"\n231\t    format_rule: engineering-guideline-format\n232\t    required_fields:\n233\t      - id\n234\t      - title\n235\t      - introduction (multi-line text)\n236\t      - sections (list with title and content)\n237\t    optional_fields:\n238\t      - references\n239\t    purpose: |\n240\t      Documents conventions, practices, and patterns above the code and\n241\t      architecture. Not SRDs (no numbered requirements). Not architecture (no\n242\t      component descriptions).\n243\t\n244\t  interface_specification:\n245\t    location: \"docs/interfaces/ifc-[kebab-case-name].yaml\"\n246\t    format_rule: interface-specification-format\n247\t    required_fields:\n248\t      - id (matches filename without extension)\n249\t      - name (must match ARCHITECTURE.yaml interface entry)\n250\t      - summary (one to three sentences)\n251\t    optional_fields:\n252\t      - \"data_structures (list: name, description, fields as typed field list)\"\n253\t      - \"operations (list: name, description, parameters, returns)\"\n254\t      - announcements (list of events the interface emits)\n255\t      - references (list of SRD or use case IDs)\n256\t    purpose: |\n257\t      Standalone, language-agnostic interface contract. Uses typed field\n258\t      lists for data structures and typed parameter/return lists for\n259\t      operations. Pure YAML throughout, consistent with D2. Replaces\n260\t      Go-specific signatures inlined in ARCHITECTURE.yaml with\n261\t      machine-parseable definitions. See eng10-interface-specifications for\n262\t      the full format and examples.\n263\t\n264\t  specification:\n265\t    location: docs/SPECIFICATIONS.yaml\n266\t    format_rule: specification-format\n267\t    required_fields:\n268\t      - id\n269\t      - title\n270\t      - overview (multi-line text)\n271\t      - \"roadmap_summary (list: version, name, use_cases_done, use_cases_total, status)\"\n272\t      - \"srd_index (list: id, title, summary, path)\"\n273\t      - \"use_case_index (list: id, title, release, status, test_suite, path)\"\n274\t      - \"test_suite_index (list: id, title, traces, test_case_count, path)\"\n275\t      - \"srd_to_use_case_mapping (list: use_case, srd, why_required, coverage)\"\n276\t      - coverage_gaps (list or multi-line text)\n277\t    optional_fields:\n278\t      - traceability_diagram (Mermaid as multi-line string)\n279\t      - references\n280\t    purpose: |\n281\t      Human-readable summary tying together SRDs, use cases, test suites, and\n282\t      roadmap into one navigable page. Does not duplicate content; summarizes\n283\t      and shows relationships. Regenerate when any artifact changes.\n284\t\n285\t  roadmap:\n286\t    location: docs/road-map.yaml\n287\t    purpose: |\n288\t      Defines releases with version, name, focus, and list of use cases with\n289\t      status. Use cases are assigned to releases. Release numbering: rel[NN].[N]\n290\t      (major.minor). Minor releases validate completed major releases.\n291\t\n292\t  generation_run_report:\n293\t    location: \"docs/reports/generation-run-[N].md (e.g., generation-run-15.md)\"\n294\t    format_rule: generation-run-report-format\n295\t    required_fields:\n296\t      - \"title: 'Generation Run [N]: [total LOC] LOC, $[total cost] — [one-line summary]'\"\n297\t      - \"Configuration section: generation branch name, base branch, cobbler version,\n298\t          model, max_turns, preserve_sources flag, issues_repo\"\n299\t      - \"Cycle-by-cycle results section: table with columns cycle, issue title, status\n300\t          (pass/fail/restart), stitch turns used, input tokens, output tokens,\n301\t          cost USD, LOC delta\"\n302\t      - \"Measure cycles section: table with columns cycle, issues proposed, input\n303\t          tokens, output tokens, cost USD\"\n304\t      - \"Cost analysis section: total input tokens, total output tokens, total cost USD,\n305\t          total LOC produced, cost per KLOC; comparison table against prior runs\n306\t          (run number, date, total LOC, total cost, cost/KLOC)\"\n307\t      - \"Generated packages section: list of packages or modules produced with their LOC\"\n308\t      - \"Failures and restarts section: for each failed stitch, the issue title, error\n309\t          summary, and whether it was retried or abandoned\"\n310\t    purpose: |\n311\t      Captures the full accounting of a completed generation run so that cost\n312\t      trends, failure patterns, and per-package productivity can be tracked\n313\t      across runs. Written once per generation after generator:stop completes.\n314\t\n315\t      Data source: before writing the report, read all history files from the\n316\t      generation branch's merged tag (.cobbler/history/ under the\n317\t      <generation-branch>-merged git tag): *-stitch-stats.yaml,\n318\t      *-measure-stats.yaml, and *-stitch-report.yaml. These files contain\n319\t      per-cycle token counts, costs, LOC deltas, and failure records.\n320\t      Do not estimate; read the files.\n321\t\n322\tnaming_conventions:\n323\t  srd: \"srd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml (e.g., srd001-cupboard-core.yaml)\"\n324\t  use_case: \"rel[NN].[N]-uc[NNN]-[short-name].yaml (e.g., rel01.0-uc001-cupboard-lifecycle.yaml)\"\n325\t  test_suite: \"test-[use-case-id].yaml (e.g., test-rel01.0-uc001-cupboard-lifecycle.yaml)\"\n326\t  engineering_guideline: \"eng[NN]-[short-name].yaml (e.g., eng01-git-integration.yaml)\"\n327\t  interface_specification: \"ifc-[kebab-case-name].yaml (e.g., ifc-generation-lifecycle.yaml)\"\n328\t  architecture: \"ARCHITECTURE.yaml\"\n329\t  vision: \"VISION.yaml\"\n330\t  specification: \"SPECIFICATIONS.yaml\"\n331\t\n332\twriting_guidelines:\n333\t  audience: Developers, agents, reviewers. Assume readers need to build or extend the system.\n334\t  tone: \"Use 'we' in active voice. Follow documentation-standards: concise, active voice, no forbidden terms.\"\n335\t  scope: Describe structure and contracts; do not duplicate. Point to upstream docs for detail.\n336\t  tables: Name all tables per documentation-standards.\n337\t  figures: Per documentation-standards. Define Mermaid inline; do not create separate image files.\n338\t\n339\ttraceability_model:\n340\t  vision: Goals and boundaries\n341\t  architecture: Components and interfaces (implements vision)\n342\t  srds: Numbered requirements (architecture points to SRDs for detail)\n343\t  use_cases: Tracer bullets (exercise architecture components via touchpoints)\n344\t  test_suites: Validation (validate use case success criteria)\n345\t  code: Implementation (traces to SRDs via commit messages)\n346\t\n347\tcompleteness_checklists:\n348\t  vision:\n349\t    - id matches project name\n350\t    - executive_summary states what the project is and what it is not\n351\t    - problem covers context, why current solutions fall short, what this does differently\n352\t    - success_criteria covers meaningful dimensions\n353\t    - implementation_phases lists phases with focus and deliverables\n354\t    - risks lists known risks with impact, likelihood, mitigation\n355\t    - not lists explicit boundaries\n356\t    - File saved as VISION.yaml in docs/\n357\t\n358\t  architecture:\n359\t    - id matches project name\n360\t    - overview.summary states what the system does and core insight\n361\t    - interfaces list data structures, operations, announcements; link to SRD for full spec\n362\t    - components list each major component with responsibility; link to SRD or use case\n363\t    - design_decisions are numbered with decision statement and benefits\n364\t    - project_structure shows directory paths and roles\n365\t    - File saved as ARCHITECTURE.yaml in docs/\n366\t\n367\t  srd:\n368\t    - id matches filename without extension\n369\t    - problem states what we solve and why it matters\n370\t    - goals are numbered (G1, G2, ...) and measurable\n371\t    - requirements are grouped (R1, R2, ...) with numbered items (R1.1, R1.2, ...)\n372\t    - Each requirement is specific and actionable\n373\t    - non_goals define what is out of scope\n374\t    - acceptance_criteria are structured objects with id, criterion, and traces\n375\t    - Every R-item appears in at least one AC's traces list\n376\t    - Interactive requirements (prompting, confirmation) specify exact prompt text, accepted input values, case sensitivity, retry behavior on invalid input, and default behavior when stdin is not a terminal\n377\t    - File saved as srd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml\n378\t\n379\t  use_case:\n380\t    - id matches filename without extension\n381\t    - flow steps are numbered (F1, F2, ...), end-to-end, map to real components\n382\t    - touchpoints are numbered (T1, T2, ...) and list interfaces, components, protocols\n383\t    - touchpoints that cite SRDs must include R-group references (e.g., \"srd030-md5sum R1, R2, R3\"); bare SRD citations without R-groups break codereadiness computation\n384\t    - success_criteria are structured objects with id, criterion, and traces to SRD AC IDs\n385\t    - test_suite references a corresponding test suite ID\n386\t    - Test suite YAML exists and traces back to this use case\n387\t    - Use case added to appropriate release in road-map.yaml\n388\t    - File saved as rel[NN].[N]-uc[NNN]-[short-name].yaml\n389\t\n390\t  test_suite:\n391\t    - id matches filename\n392\t    - traces lists at least one use case or SRD requirement\n393\t    - preconditions describe shared starting state\n394\t    - Each test case has name, inputs, expected outputs\n395\t    - Inputs use real commands and data\n396\t    - Expected outputs are specific and checkable\n397\t    - File saved as test-[use-case-id].yaml\n398\t\n399\t  interface_specification:\n400\t    - id matches filename without extension (ifc-[kebab-case-name])\n401\t    - name matches the corresponding ARCHITECTURE.yaml interface entry\n402\t    - summary describes the interface contract in one to three sentences\n403\t    - data_structures use typed field lists (name, type, description, required)\n404\t    - operations have name, description, typed parameters, and typed returns\n405\t    - references list SRD or use case IDs the interface traces to\n406\t    - File saved as ifc-[kebab-case-name].yaml in docs/interfaces/\n407\t\n408\tsections:\n409\t  - tag: articles\n410\t    title: Core Principles\n411\t    content: |\n412\t      Five principles govern the design phase: specifications are the source of\n413\t      truth, YAML is the format for structured documents, every use case must\n414\t      link to a test suite, all artifacts trace through a chain from vision to\n415\t      code, and releases are roadmap-driven.\n416\t  - tag: documentation_standards\n417\t    title: Documentation Standards\n418\t    content: |\n419\t      Write in the style of Strunk and White: concise, active voice, specific,\n420\t      no unnecessary words. Use the royal \"we.\" Tables over lists for short\n421\t      entries. Avoid forbidden terms such as \"critical,\" \"key,\" and \"strategic.\"\n422\t  - tag: document_types\n423\t    title: Document Types\n424\t    content: |\n425\t      Eight document types are defined: vision, architecture, SRD, use case,\n426\t      test suite, engineering guideline, interface specification, and\n427\t      specification. Each has a canonical location, format rule, required\n428\t      fields, and optional fields.\n429\t  - tag: naming_conventions\n430\t    title: Naming Conventions\n431\t    content: |\n432\t      File names follow strict patterns per document type (e.g.,\n433\t      srd[NNN]-[feature].yaml, rel[NN].[N]-uc[NNN]-[name].yaml). Use lowercase\n434\t      kebab-case throughout.\n435\t  - tag: writing_guidelines\n436\t    title: Writing Guidelines\n437\t    content: |\n438\t      Write for developers, agents, and reviewers. Describe structure and\n439\t      contracts; point to upstream docs for detail. Use \"we\" in active voice and\n440\t      present tense.\n441\t  - tag: traceability_model\n442\t    title: Traceability Model\n443\t    content: |\n444\t      Every artifact traces through the chain: vision goals → architecture\n445\t      components → SRD requirements → use case tracer bullets → test suite\n446\t      validation → code implementation.\n447\t  - tag: completeness_checklists\n448\t    title: Completeness Checklists\n449\t    content: |\n450\t      Each document type has a checklist of required fields and cross-references\n451\t      to verify before closing an issue. Run mage analyze to catch missing\n452\t      linkages automatically.\n453\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwpTYm5bvMN9bbibU2HZ3B","session_id":"4766f4dd-6cc1-473e-8675-aaab6bf559a6","uuid":"79287c7b-80c2-46be-936a-63277887fd33","timestamp":"2026-05-28T19:21:13.779Z"}
