{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01XmeGhzRtTj3cLEP9qPJoSE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012fMsCtgKbMQAwBa5rwFnZr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5641/docs/constitutions/execution.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9700,"cache_read_input_tokens":27942,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9700,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwpTYm5bvMN9bbibU2HZ3B","session_id":"4766f4dd-6cc1-473e-8675-aaab6bf559a6","uuid":"bced8d93-5a53-49f0-b72e-cf7c7c7de1db"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012fMsCtgKbMQAwBa5rwFnZr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Execution Constitution\n2\t#\n3\t# This constitution governs the stitch phase: implementing a single task from\n4\t# the work queue. It is injected into the stitch prompt so Claude knows how to\n5\t# write code that matches project conventions and quality standards.\n6\t\n7\tarticles:\n8\t  - id: E1\n9\t    title: Specification-first\n10\t    rule: |\n11\t      Code must correspond to existing SRDs and architecture. Read the SRDs and\n12\t      ARCHITECTURE sections listed in Required Reading before writing code. Do\n13\t      not invent interfaces, types, or patterns not described in those docs.\n14\t\n15\t  - id: E2\n16\t    title: Traceability\n17\t    rule: |\n18\t      Commit messages must mention which SRDs (or aspects) are implemented.\n19\t      Example: \"Implement X (srd-feature-name R6-R7)\". Where useful (package or\n20\t      top-of-file comments), list the implemented SRDs.\n21\t\n22\t  - id: E3\n23\t    title: No scope creep\n24\t    rule: |\n25\t      Do NOT modify files outside the Files to Create/Modify list unless a\n26\t      requirement explicitly demands it. Do NOT add features, refactoring, or\n27\t      improvements beyond what the requirements specify. Complete the task as\n28\t      scoped, nothing more.\n29\t\n30\t  - id: E4\n31\t    title: Session completion\n32\t    rule: |\n33\t      Work is complete when all files are written and tests pass. Do NOT run\n34\t      any git commands. Git operations (add, commit, status, init) are handled\n35\t      externally by the orchestrator.\n36\t\n37\t  - id: E5\n38\t    title: Quality gates\n39\t    rule: |\n40\t      Every item in Acceptance Criteria must be verified. Run tests if the\n41\t      criteria require it. Do not skip any criterion.\n42\t\n43\t  - id: E6\n44\t    title: Non-goals enforcement\n45\t    rule: |\n46\t      Features listed in a SRD's non_goals must not be implemented. Do not\n47\t      parse, accept, or act on flags, options, or behaviors that appear in\n48\t      non_goals. If a requirement references a non_goal feature, treat it as\n49\t      a specification error and skip the requirement with a TODO comment\n50\t      citing the conflict.\n51\t\n52\t  - id: E7\n53\t    title: Skip ambiguous requirements\n54\t    rule: |\n55\t      If a requirement is too vague to implement completely, do not implement\n56\t      it partially. Skip the requirement entirely. Add a TODO comment citing\n57\t      the requirement ID and describing what is missing from the specification.\n58\t      A parsed-but-nonfunctional flag is worse than a missing flag.\n59\t\n60\t  - id: E8\n61\t    title: Function size limit\n62\t    rule: |\n63\t      No function may exceed 40 lines of code. This is a hard limit, not a\n64\t      guideline. If a function exceeds 40 lines, find a seam and split it\n65\t      before the task is considered complete. Common seams: validation logic,\n66\t      formatting logic, I/O operations, and branch-specific handling.\n67\t\n68\t  - id: E9\n69\t    title: File size limit\n70\t    rule: |\n71\t      Each Go source file should stay under 500 lines of production code.\n72\t      When a file exceeds this threshold, split by responsibility: main.go\n73\t      (entry point and arg parsing), format.go (output formatting), and\n74\t      additional files as needed. Test files follow the same split. pkg/\n75\t      packages follow the same 500-line threshold.\n76\t\n77\t  - id: E10\n78\t    title: DRY enforcement\n79\t    rule: |\n80\t      The duplication threshold is two. If you write the same logic twice,\n81\t      extract it into a shared function. Common violations: entry display\n82\t      logic duplicated across output modes, error formatting repeated in\n83\t      multiple functions, path construction logic copied between list and\n84\t      recurse paths. Before writing a function, search for existing code\n85\t      that does the same thing.\n86\t\n87\tcoding_standards:\n88\t  copyright_header: |\n89\t    Every Go file must start with the SPDX copyright header:\n90\t    // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n91\t    // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n92\t\n93\t  never_duplicate_code: |\n94\t    Before writing a function, search for existing code that does the same thing.\n95\t    When two pieces of code share logic, extract the common part. The threshold\n96\t    is two: if you write the same thing twice, extract it. Common violations:\n97\t    entry display logic duplicated across output modes, error formatting repeated\n98\t    in multiple functions, path construction logic copied between list and recurse\n99\t    paths. See article E10.\n100\t\n101\t  design_patterns:\n102\t    - Strategy: Multiple implementations, caller picks one. Define interface, not if/else ladder.\n103\t    - Command: Encapsulate actions for queuing, undo, or composition.\n104\t    - Facade: Simplified interface to complex subsystem. No toggle parameters.\n105\t    - Factory: Centralized construction via NewXxx() functions.\n106\t    - Decorator: Add cross-cutting concerns (logging, timing) without modifying the wrapped object.\n107\t    - Adapter: Interface translation for foreign APIs.\n108\t\n109\t  interfaces: |\n110\t    Introduce an interface when you have two concrete implementations or when\n111\t    you need to mock a dependency in tests. Do not create an interface for a\n112\t    single implementation \"just in case.\" Accept interfaces as parameters;\n113\t    return concrete structs. Keep interfaces small: one to three methods.\n114\t\n115\t  struct_and_function_design: |\n116\t    Each struct represents one concept. Each function does one thing. If a\n117\t    function takes more than three parameters, group related parameters into a\n118\t    config struct. No function may exceed 40 lines — this is a hard limit\n119\t    enforced by article E8. Common seams for splitting: validation, formatting,\n120\t    I/O, and branch-specific handling.\n121\t\n122\t  error_handling: |\n123\t    Handle errors at the point they occur. Use guard clauses: check err != nil\n124\t    and return early. Wrap errors with context: fmt.Errorf(\"doing X: %w\", err).\n125\t    Never silently discard an error with _ unless the operation is best-effort\n126\t    cleanup (e.g., removing a temp file). When discarding, leave a comment.\n127\t\n128\t  no_magic_strings: |\n129\t    Centralize all string literals that name external binaries, file paths,\n130\t    URLs, or repeated text. Binary names: const (e.g., binGit, binGo). Large\n131\t    prompts: embedded .tmpl templates. Never scatter raw string literals.\n132\t\n133\t  project_structure: |\n134\t    cmd/: Entry points. Minimal: parse flags, wire dependencies, start.\n135\t    internal/: Private implementation. Not importable outside this module.\n136\t    pkg/: Shared public types and interfaces. No implementation.\n137\t    tests/: Integration tests.\n138\t    magefiles/: Build tooling. Flat directory. One file per concern.\n139\t\n140\t    Align package structure to SRD component structure. Each major component\n141\t    maps to one package. Avoid package names like util, common, helpers.\n142\t\n143\t  standard_packages:\n144\t    - \"Build automation: magefile/mage\"\n145\t    - \"CLI framework: spf13/cobra\"\n146\t    - \"Configuration: spf13/viper\"\n147\t    - \"Testing assertions: stretchr/testify\"\n148\t    - \"YAML parsing: gopkg.in/yaml.v3\"\n149\t    - \"JSON handling: encoding/json (stdlib)\"\n150\t\n151\t  naming_conventions:\n152\t    exported: PascalCase (e.g., CupboardConfig)\n153\t    unexported: camelCase (e.g., cobblerConfig)\n154\t    cli_flags: kebab-case (e.g., --silence-agent)\n155\t    binary_constants: \"bin prefix + PascalCase (e.g., binGit, binClaude)\"\n156\t    factories: \"New prefix (e.g., NewBackend())\"\n157\t    interfaces: \"Action or capability (e.g., Table, Reader)\"\n158\t\n159\t  concurrency: |\n160\t    Pass context.Context as the first parameter to any function that does I/O\n161\t    or may block. Never start a goroutine without a plan for how it exits. Use\n162\t    sync.WaitGroup or a done channel to manage lifetimes.\n163\t\n164\t  testing: |\n165\t    Every exported function and every meaningful branch deserves a test. Use\n166\t    table-driven parameterized tests for similar cases. Extract shared setup\n167\t    into test helpers. Build reusable test utilities in a testutil package.\n168\t\n169\ttraceability:\n170\t  before_implementing:\n171\t    - Identify related SRDs, ARCHITECTURE, use cases, test suites, guidelines, VISION\n172\t    - Read the relevant sections so behavior, data shapes, and contracts are clear\n173\t    - Implement so the code conforms to the requirements and design described\n174\t\n175\t  commit_message: |\n176\t    Must mention which SRDs (or aspects) are being implemented. Prefer explicit:\n177\t    \"Implement X (srd-feature-name, srd-component)\" or \"Add Y per srd-feature R12\".\n178\t    If only parts touched, say so: \"Implement operation X (srd-feature R8, R13)\".\n179\t\n180\t  code_comments: |\n181\t    At the top of a file or package doc, list implemented SRDs and architecture\n182\t    sections. Do not repeat this in every function; use file- or package-level\n183\t    comments only.\n184\t\n185\t  reference_paths:\n186\t    - \"Architecture: docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml\"\n187\t    - \"SRDs: docs/specs/software-requirements/srd*.yaml\"\n188\t    - \"Use cases: docs/specs/use-cases/rel*-uc*-*.yaml\"\n189\t    - \"Test suites (YAML specs): docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-*.yaml\"\n190\t    - \"Release tests (Go): tests/rel-*/rel-*_test.go\"\n191\t    - \"Engineering guidelines: docs/engineering/eng*.md\"\n192\t    - \"Vision: docs/VISION.yaml\"\n193\t\n194\tbuild_and_test:\n195\t  build_within_module: |\n196\t    Always build and test from within the Go module directory. Do not cd\n197\t    outside the module or create separate Go modules for testing. Use\n198\t    `go build -o bin/<name> ./cmd/<name>/` for cmd/ binaries so outputs\n199\t    land in bin/ (git-ignored). Do not use mage build or mage lint for\n200\t    individual packages — use go build and go vet directly.\n201\t\n202\t  test_planning: |\n203\t    Plan test prerequisites before writing test code. Determine what\n204\t    binaries, fixtures, or reference implementations the tests need.\n205\t    If cmd/ is empty during generation (code not yet written), skip\n206\t    tests that require a built binary — use t.Skip with an explanatory\n207\t    message. For tests that compare against reference binaries (e.g.\n208\t    GNU coreutils), always use exec.LookPath and t.Skip if the\n209\t    reference is not installed.\n210\t\n211\t  negative_tests: |\n212\t    Do not write subtests that intentionally fail the test harness and\n213\t    then fix them afterward. Every test you write must pass on the first\n214\t    run. Use assertions (require.Equal, assert.Error) to check expected\n215\t    error conditions. Use subprocess exec.Command to test exit codes\n216\t    and signal handling rather than calling os.Exit in-process. Never\n217\t    write a test that you expect to fail — if behavior is conditional,\n218\t    use t.Skip or build tags.\n219\t\n220\t  repository_notes: |\n221\t    Files with \"collision\" in the name are test fixtures for hash\n222\t    collision testing — do not delete or modify them. Utilities that\n223\t    write to stdout must install a SIGPIPE handler (signal.Notify +\n224\t    signal.Reset) so piped output does not cause a broken-pipe crash.\n225\t\n226\tsession_completion:\n227\t  git_managed_externally: true  # Do NOT run any git commands\n228\t  token_tracking: true  # Log tokens used per issue\n229\t\n230\t  workflow:\n231\t    - Run quality gates if code changed (tests, linters, builds)\n232\t    - Ensure all files are written and saved\n233\t    - Verify code compiles and tests pass\n234\t\n235\t  critical_rules:\n236\t    - Do NOT run any git commands (add, commit, status, init, rm .git)\n237\t    - Git is managed externally by the orchestrator\n238\t    - Your job is to write code and verify it works\n239\t    - Do NOT use bd or cupboard commands\n240\t\n241\ttechnology:\n242\t  primary_language: Go\n243\t  python_manager: pixi  # Use pixi for Python, not pip/pip3 directly\n244\t  cli_framework: cobra\n245\t  build_system: mage\n246\t  yaml_library: gopkg.in/yaml.v3\n247\t  issue_tracker: cupboard\n248\t\n249\tgit_conventions:\n250\t  note: Git is managed externally. Do NOT run any git commands.\n251\t\n252\tsections:\n253\t  - tag: articles\n254\t    title: Core Principles\n255\t    content: |\n256\t      Ten principles govern the stitch phase: specification-first development,\n257\t      commit traceability, no scope creep, session completion via\n258\t      orchestrator-managed git, quality gate enforcement, non-goals enforcement,\n259\t      skip-on-ambiguity, function size limits, file size limits, and DRY\n260\t      enforcement.\n261\t  - tag: coding_standards\n262\t    title: Coding Standards\n263\t    content: |\n264\t      Go code follows the project's style guide: typed error handling, no magic\n265\t      strings, one concept per struct, and design patterns applied to eliminate\n266\t      conditional branching. Interfaces are introduced only when two concrete\n267\t      implementations exist.\n268\t  - tag: traceability\n269\t    title: Traceability\n270\t    content: |\n271\t      Before implementing, read the specified SRDs and architecture sections.\n272\t      Commit messages must cite SRDs. Add a SRD list to file or package comments\n273\t      where useful.\n274\t  - tag: session_completion\n275\t    title: Session Completion\n276\t    content: |\n277\t      Work is complete when all files are written and tests pass. Git operations\n278\t      are managed externally by the orchestrator; do not run any git commands.\n279\t  - tag: technology\n280\t    title: Technology Stack\n281\t    content: |\n282\t      The project uses Go with mage for build automation, cobra for CLI, viper\n283\t      for configuration, yaml.v3 for YAML parsing, and cupboard for issue tracking.\n284\t  - tag: git_conventions\n285\t    title: Git Conventions\n286\t    content: |\n287\t      Git is managed externally. Do not run any git commands during a stitch\n288\t      session; the orchestrator handles all staging, committing, and branching.\n289\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwpTYm5bvMN9bbibU2HZ3B","session_id":"4766f4dd-6cc1-473e-8675-aaab6bf559a6","uuid":"7f33d9ba-45d9-4a59-b929-602b9a427eb6","timestamp":"2026-05-28T19:21:16.413Z"}
