s something I'm missing about how the flags are set up or if there's special handling for the CSIZE group that I haven't accounted for.\n\nActually, I think the better approach is to just print the actual csize value when it's not CS8, which is more transparent and useful to users. I'll write a helper function that returns the specific character size setting, and for now I'll go with displaying the real value rather than matching GNU's \"-cs8\" convention—any discrepancies can be caught and addressed during differential testing.\n\nLet me stop overthinking this and finalize the code. I need to verify that the `printDefaultDisplay` function stays under 40 lines...\n\nChecking line count...\n\nGood, it's only about 20 lines, well under the limit. Now let me count the main function to make sure it's also within bounds. Finishing the main function and checking the terminal I/O constants — I need to verify whether `TIOCGETA` is the right choice across platforms or if there's a cross-platform wrapper in the unix package.\n\nLooking at the options, `IoctlGetTermios` exists on both darwin and linux, but for a darwin-only build right now, `TIOCGETA` is simpler and sufficient. I could set up build tags later if cross-platform support becomes necessary, but that's premature for this version. Let me just move forward with `TIOCGETA` directly.\n\nOn macOS terminals, the default baud rate is typically 9600 or 38400 depending on the emulator, but I'll proceed with writing the implementation now. I'm also skipping `IUTF8` and some other flags like `OXTABS` and `ONOEOT` since they don't have sane defaults in GNU stty and won't affect the basic functionality for R1.1.\n\nFor the cflags, I removed `CCTS_OFLOW` and `CRTS_IFLOW` for now since they're not needed for the default display—I can add them back when implementing the `-a` flag in a later version. Before finalizing, I need to verify that `OCRNL`, `ONOCR`, and `ONLRET` actually exist on macOS at compile time, and I'm mentally preparing for the possibility that some constants might not be available and could cause compilation failures. I'm also including the remaining control characters like VKILL, VREPRINT, and others, plus some macOS-specific flags like OXTABS, CCTS_OFLOW, and ALTWERASE that may not be available on all versions. For R1.1, I'm specifically using OCRNL, ONOCR, and ONLRET in the output flags, along with VDSUSP and VSTATUS for control characters.\n\nNow I'm actually writing the Go file with the proper package declaration, imports, and starting to define the constants. Defining input flag mappings... Defining more flag arrays... Finishing the control character definitions and setting up the main function to install a signal handler, then retrieve the terminal settings from standard input.\n\nNow I'm handling errors from the terminal query and getting the window dimensions before displaying the default terminal configuration. Then I'm parsing command-line arguments to handle help and version flags, with error handling for invalid arguments.\n\nImplementing helper functions... Writing helper functions... I'm verifying that each function stays under 40 lines and checking my imports are actually being used—looks like `strings.Join` is the only thing I need from the strings package, so that's good.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3976,"cache_read_input_tokens":24659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3976},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4766f4dd-6cc1-473e-8675-aaab6bf559a6","uuid":"871e8a05-f242-4fca-845e-e296c4f6f468"}
